WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.180
Guerrilla War, your small island country is struggling against the cruel domination of an evil dictator.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:29.760
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:30.760
I'm Mike.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.400
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.440
And I'm here too.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.840
All right. You gave us a little scare there for a second.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.480
I wanted people to think it was going to be another Sean and Mike exclusive interview.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.260
Well, the problem there is so many people wrote in and said that if Joe doesn't come back for this next episode, they're canceling their subscription.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.280
I don't believe it.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.620
You should. Yeah, you should believe it.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:01:01.180
And by canceling their subscription, I mean the completely free button that you click subscribe to make sure that you get new episodes every Friday, just like this one.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.480
But I save all that, you know, fancy marketing talk for the end of the episode.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.700
Guys, we're talking about Guerrilla War.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.700
Before we start talking about this game, I just want to mention we're back to war games.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.720
And honestly, we have back to back war games here, too.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.820
What makes like war so natural for video games?

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.360
Wait, so you really don't have much to talk about.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:25.860
I don't think so.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.440
We're talking about war.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:33.520
What makes like war, especially, you know, like, look, you know, it's NES, it's the NES days, right?

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:41.640
So like you don't have the information of like, well, you know, like Call of Duty and Counter-Strike are going to be like huge, like, you know, million dollar makers for those companies.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.800
And they're going to continue to release iterations upon iterations of battlefields and stuff like that.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.880
Right now you're just like starting out.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:54.680
So what makes war be like a thing that's like, yeah, we're going to make war video games, not more like Pac-Man.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.800
Yeah, I mean, there's usually two sides.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:04.720
So a protagonist and an antagonist, depending on, you know, who you're fighting for.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.280
There are win and lose conditions.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:14.020
You are motivated to act in the best interest of, you know, defeating the enemy.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:18.160
It's pretty natural, like in terms of game mechanics.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.860
Yeah, I feel like it's like the easiest form of like a high stakes conflict.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:28.960
I mean, the practicing, like military practices and maneuvers are literally called war games.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.280
So it's, yeah, it's natural.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:41.680
And so, so far on the NES, we've had quite a few war games here, but we've also had quite a game, quite a few games that have played just like Guerrilla War.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:47.120
Do you guys remember stuff like Ikari Warriors, Commando, games like those?

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.260
Yeah, loved Commando.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.460
And then like Ikari Warriors 2 took that weird path where it was like in space instead.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.100
But we're just going to disregard that one.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.760
We didn't give it a great review either.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.620
Joe, you said you loved Commando.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:06.020
Do you, did you just love this style of video game or was there something about Commando that was calling you?

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:17.920
This is something that I was going to get into at some point here too, but I did love Commando, I think because it was the first, it was, I think it was our first real experience on the NES with this style of video game.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:30.660
And it partially brought me back to some NES things and it also just, you know, I don't want to say impressed me, but like a little bit impressed me that it was, you know, a fun NES game in this like such an old retro thing.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:38.620
I do think that as time has gone on and we've played more of these, you know, it takes more to impress me with one of these games.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:47.720
And I'm sure if I went back to Commando, I wouldn't be as impressed either because now I see like other things that could be done on the NES that might make a game more robust.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:55.640
So, so like this game definitely had a higher bar, like had a higher bar to meet for me than Commando.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:04.520
That's sort of how it goes, I think, with most like late comers to a genre that we've been interacting with in this podcast.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:09.060
Like the first one, all you really got to do is not be broken, right?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:10.200
Right, exactly.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:19.260
There's, there's something funny about going back and playing those older NES titles that are really not even that old to us, right?

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.300
We're only on episode 187 here.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:27.720
But it's funny to think like after I played Guerrilla War, I was like, oh, I should go check out these older games.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:35.820
And just loading up Ikari Warriors, like that was a trip, you know, forget about Commando, which is even further back.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:48.940
But I couldn't believe that just the difference in the action component of the game, like how, how fast it all happens, how good it feels to be your character, how smooth the motion is.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:56.480
Because we kind of take for granted as we play through this chronologically, like it takes us a long time to go from like month to month to month.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:10.800
But if you ever like jump back and play some of these older games that we didn't review so kindly, it's really crazy to see how quickly developers have figured out the NES from, you know, 1986 over to 1989.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:21.360
Yeah, this definitely feels very different and a bit more polished because I was not a fan of Commando or Ikari Warriors.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.520
But I definitely felt a little bit different about this one.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:31.840
Yeah, I think it like what resonates with me the most is like the smooth, the smoothness of the way you move.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.480
I haven't played Commando, I mean, in a long time.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.660
But if I remember correctly, it does not move as smooth as this game.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.320
And then you go back?

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:45.420
I mean, that's the, that's the one, Joe, that you, you know, that's where the joke started of watch out for that bullet.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:46.500
Yeah, that's right.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:02.380
Before we get into like the gameplay, though, can we go back to like the, the setup for this game?

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.020
Well, which version do you want to go with?

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.040
Because, you know, we had this issue last, last episode, too.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:20.700
Keep in mind, these are two different developers, which always makes me think when these coincidences happen so close together, that it's really Nintendo of America strategically changing and releasing these games for the Western audience.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:32.220
But I'm assuming that you want to talk about the original Famicom version of Guerrilla War and what that one had you, who you were playing as and where, where it was taking place.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:38.340
Absolutely. And it's sort of this game's claim to fame since it, I had never heard of it before now.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:43.960
But like whenever I have read about people talk, talk about this game, this is really all it's known for.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:59.640
So it's Cuba, like this, you know, the small island nation that you are liberating is just Cuba and you are either Chi Guevara or you are a follower of Chi Guevara.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:05.300
And I'm just amazed that in this game you play as communist revolutionaries.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:13.300
And even though they they didn't come out and say it like the Japanese version, where I think it was actually literally named Guevara.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.840
Yeah. It's so obvious.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:26.340
And I just think that's so funny for late 80s peak, not peak Cold War, but we're still in the Cold War.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.880
It's it's so funny.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:30.780
And it's it's even better.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:43.520
It's it's I think that this one, it makes more sense that they were trying to censor it than the last game, because the last game it was just, you know, World War Two, the most good versus bad war you can think of.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:50.180
This one was definitely a bit more prudent of a decision to not tell kids you're a communist revolutionary.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:57.200
Yeah, Sean, you're spot on there, because like this is something that you would think NBC News would have just loved to run with.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.840
But I can't find any stories about it.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:05.640
But like they would they would be like, you know, video games are causing violence or they're stopping kids from completing their homework back then.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:09.000
Like those stories go all the way back to the launch of the NES.

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:12.020
They're not just like news stories that were developed in like the last 10 years.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:25.140
And yet no one's talking about the fact that like our kids are being indoctrinated by a game that claims to be about a fictitious island similar to Cuba, where communists win, you know, and meanwhile, you're playing as them.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:40.220
But I think, you know, there's something with this one that with the censorship that I don't think it's like, I don't know, like technically like commando, right?

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:50.000
You could have also put in or, you know, Akari Warriors, like you could put in what country they're from and what soldiers you are like based on real life soldiers.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.980
And it would be controversial.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.380
It just happens to be that this one did claim that at one point and then took it out.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:07.160
Yeah, I think that even with Akari Warriors and commando, it was pretty obvious that you were in a Vietnam analog.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:14.800
And that's sort of like the entire opposite, like you're an American commando fighting against communist forces.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.960
And I think that it was a bit more generic.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:31.220
And because like Vietnam is it's not as well tread as World War Two in terms of setting for a war video game.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:40.200
It was common enough that it like it's not very it's not very controversial, even though it may have been in the first place.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:46.300
Whereas like this is the only game I can think of that takes place during the Cuban Revolution.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:48.280
Sure. Yeah.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:55.840
It's also I think like a you know, if you think about it, like the context of today, people would know what this game was about for sure.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:10:01.040
But like everyone on the the the audience on the NES was mostly like children.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:05.300
It wasn't like today where like the the main audience of like video games is like 30 plus.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:09.800
So like a lot of the people playing this game were probably like 10 years old and it might have gone like they might have just.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.940
Yeah, you know, it might have gone right over their head.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.800
They just bought right into like a country is struggling against the domination of a king.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:22.760
And like not picked up and you know, like not picked up any of those references and the parents never watched her.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:28.180
So like maybe that's why there's no coverage or there was no there were no there was no outrage or stories about it or anything.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:40.180
I just want to be I just want to go back to 1989 and be like a parent just sort of like crossing through like crossing through the the living room or the family room.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:48.400
Where the kids playing a game and just like catching a glimpse of that low low res picture of Chicavera that like they didn't change.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:50.740
Like right. Is that Chicavera?

00:10:52.680 --> 00:11:00.640
Yeah, that's the one thing that I think would like would be the the like the flag that people would notice like if they were walking by the rest of the game.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.360
If you're just walking by you're not even notice it.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:04.560
But they got this one image at the at the top.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:13.960
Uh-huh. One more thing before we get into the nooks and crannies of the game itself is that, you know, because we're talking so much about Ikari Warriors and Commando.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:19.420
You have to remember those two games Ikari Warriors was also developed by SNK, which this game was developed by.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:22.240
But Commando was developed by Capcom.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:28.260
Those two games were also arcade games that were very popular and then ported to the NES.

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.840
Guerrilla War has the same exact story.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:41.260
And I think the more interesting thing there is like there was a time on this podcast where we were always like, oh, well, you know, yeah, the arcade ports are always going to be better or what, you know, like whatever, blah, blah, blah.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:47.080
Like, yeah, I put this on the essential arcade game list, but I can't put it on the NES essential games list.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:53.960
I think here we're finally starting to see something that is a little closer to feeling like it could have been in the arcades.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.460
I'm not saying it plays as good as the arcade version.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:57.480
It definitely doesn't.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:01.200
I've checked out the arcade version and there's just a lot of improvements all around.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:13.360
But we're starting to get closer to the home console experience, feeling like whatever chips are in the NES are capable of emulating the same experience.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:14.540
Do you guys agree with that?

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:15.920
Absolutely, yeah.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:26.100
I feel like for the most part, like this is one of the few games like this so far that I don't ever really feel like I'm fighting the game itself, you know?

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:34.200
Like I feel like I'm trying, you know, what's programmed to be difficult is like what hits me.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:41.160
But I never feel like I'm like, well, the controls just don't respond enough and that's why I'm dying or anything like that.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:51.460
In fact, I feel like I could even dodge bullets more easily and it felt less ridiculous for some reason in this game just because of the way it feels.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:57.600
But I feel like I could, you know, it was like everything was a little more attainable because the controls worked better.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.100
Let's get into the game itself here, all right?

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:08.160
All right, Sean, you mentioned, you know, the environment of Cuba and stuff like that and the setting kind of being unmistakable.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:12.200
But like, you know, pretend you're even just on an unnamed Caribbean island.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:15.920
How does the environment play a role in the gameplay here?

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:26.180
So I didn't want to set the bar of this game of being like, oh, the setting is unmistakable.

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:32.700
Like it is just another like jungle fighter that eventually you go to a city.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.940
I guess I meant more in the broad strokes.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.440
It was sort of like obvious what they were doing.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:50.760
But got it. Yeah, I think that like the like at least like going into different settings, if it's not just jungle and then going into a military base,

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:59.900
like you do go in the jungle and you do go to a military base, but true to the guerrilla war title, then you go into a city.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.320
And then I guess you go into a sewer.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:10.400
But like the fact that they added the city was was definitely sort of unique to this game.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:20.880
But even like the jungle tropes themselves and stuff like that, I feel like we didn't really see it carried out in the way that guerrilla war does,

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:30.080
because like, you know, you can go in the water and now you're slower in the water and you have to deal with enemies, you know, coming in from the left and right as well.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:35.420
And then like everybody has the, you know, the trenches and you can use the trenches, too.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:40.900
There's obstacles in front that you can kind of blow up with grenades, but you couldn't like just shoot at them.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.920
They would block you from shooting other enemies.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.540
I feel like there's a lot more going on with the environments here.

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:55.060
You know, even just blocking your paths and saying like, hey, if you want to do this, like you either need a tank or you need to just go around because, you know,

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.660
there's no other way to really like confront this group of enemies.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:05.540
I feel like there's a lot going on there in the environment in a way we haven't seen in a lot of these action overhead run and gun games before.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.720
Yeah. The environment is not just like a backdrop.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:15.480
You know, as far as games that do this kind of thing, I'd put this like somewhere in the middle of like impressive,

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:23.160
but it's always nice to make sure that like it's always nice to notice that the environment affects you in some way.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.380
The trenches were a really cool touch, like you mentioned, and having some destructible stuff.

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:31.180
I actually wish that they did a little more with that since they went in that direction in the first place.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:37.760
But it was, you know, better than it just being like a mat that you run around on.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:50.080
Yeah. My favorite part of the destructible environments, even though they weren't as widespread as they would in a modern game,

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:54.900
like the fact that if you have a more powerful weapon like the flamethrower or I think,

00:15:55.680 --> 00:16:00.760
I guess it's like a high fire rate rocket launcher.

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:11.220
Sure. If you shoot it across a bridge, you will blow up the bridge and you will, it'll be slower to cross and it has a cool visual effect.

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:17.560
It definitely feels like you have more of an impact on the environment than in past games,

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:22.180
which is more just like a coat of jungle paint on a corridor shooter.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.280
Sean, you briefly mentioned the power-ups there for a minute, like the flamethrowers and stuff like that.

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.920
I guess it might be considered just an SNK trope,

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:41.280
but I was a little surprised to see that the power-up items are just designated by different letters on the screen.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:46.140
Given the amount of detail that they're pouring into like the environment and, you know,

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.940
all the different types of enemies that they've littered the screen with and all the different places you go to,

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:57.620
I just thought like, you know, it's a little weird to just make the power-up items like L, F, B, you know,

00:16:57.700 --> 00:16:59.660
like it was just strange.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.300
Yeah. The power-ups themselves, cool.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:09.940
Yeah. I guess I've seen it enough times now that I don't really question it between shmups and these top-down shooters,

00:17:10.420 --> 00:17:21.280
but, I mean, I think a little flame in that, just a little pixelized flame, like that's just as readable as an F.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:27.880
Yeah. I was going to say when I first saw those, I was kind of thinking like,

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:33.120
well, you know what, it's actually not so bad because it's so abundantly clear what you're picking up.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:34.380
But then I thought the same thing.

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:36.800
I was like, even if it wasn't a little flame,

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:40.260
even if it was a flamethrower sprite that you wouldn't know what it was right away,

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.680
when I see a power-up that says F on it,

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:45.520
I still don't know what it is until I pick it up and say, oh, it's a flamethrower.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.860
So might as well just put a flamethrower there and I'll pick it up and say, oh, it's a flamethrower.

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:50.020
F can mean fast.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:52.980
Right, yeah. F can mean fungus.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:55.840
Yeah, you could have contracted a fungus.

00:17:56.020 --> 00:18:01.660
Or it could just be, you know, Mario, like F means fungus, meaning you're going to grow like in Mario.

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:07.820
Yeah. Speaking of the enemies for a second, you know, I mentioned they're littered over the screen.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:09.100
Sometimes that's a good thing.

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:10.240
Sometimes that's a bad thing.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:14.180
I think in shmups, sometimes we wish that there were less enemies.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.460
But in other games, we say, you know, they're too easy because there's not enough of them.

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.100
Joe, where did you stand here on?

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:25.020
And don't just think of stage one, because I think stage one does introduce you to a lot of enemies all at once.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:34.300
But like just in the game as a whole, did you feel like the enemies were overwhelming or that the abundance of action was actually to its benefit?

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.440
Well, this is actually kind of what I was alluding to earlier.

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:53.060
I feel like a lot of other games like this, shmups and, you know, these like run and gun type games get really overwhelming to the point where sometimes I just feel like you can just get unlucky by being just in a cluster of projectiles and enemies.

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:59.420
Where like you have nowhere to move and like unless you're like superhuman with your reflexes, you're probably going to die.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:06.120
This felt much more, it felt still challenging to dodge things and stuff, but in a manageable way.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:13.880
Like I felt like if I really focused, I could be skilled enough to not get hit, which I know that other people probably feel that way in those other games.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:14.920
I'm just not that skilled.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:17.800
So like maybe that's the, you know, that's the difference.

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:24.520
But I felt like there was, I guess there was a easier or like a less of a barrier to entry.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:32.820
You can kind of pick this game up and get good at it pretty quickly so that by the time you get to the end of the game, which is not a very long game, you can feel like you you're good at it.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:43.340
Whereas I feel like a lot of these other games you might have to play for, you know, over and over again for a long time before you're like you've gotten good enough to like really feel like you mastered it.

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.020
Well, that's another point right there.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:56.940
Talking about difficulty.

00:19:57.340 --> 00:20:10.200
This game is also really weird in that you you basically it's a modern game design where you never lose progress like you can lose lives and then it reads out.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:11.780
This was your score.

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.580
But it doesn't warp you back to the beginning of the game.

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:17.320
It doesn't warp you back to the beginning of the level.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:24.820
It warps you to the lower part of your screen, albeit without any power ups, but you never lose progress.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.980
You can beat this game in a sitting no matter how bad you are.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:35.760
Yeah. And to be to be clear to what you're mentioning there is, in my opinion, where what I was just talking about goes too far.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.740
Yeah. And that's where I think they crossed the line and that ruins it for me.

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:49.620
But if you ignore that part, if you're like trying to not get across the line, if you're trying to not die and you're like you can get better at it that way, that's where I'm impressed.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:59.200
But yeah, what you just mentioned, I think, actually is like is like a good point in the argument against modern game design or taking it too far and crossing.

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:05.540
Well, Joe, they crossed the line there because this is a this is a relatively short game when you have that kind of power to you.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:11.320
I think in like a modern game, you know, a lot of the a lot of the game changes throughout.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:18.940
And that, you know, that makes it exciting and worth keeping going through and solving puzzles or or leveling up your character and stuff like that.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:22.560
But also, like, you know, you're talking about what could be a 16 hour game.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:24.760
You know, this is not that kind of thing.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:32.000
And so I think that's why they crossed the line here, maybe, you know, and they should have warped you back to the beginning of the stage or something instead of just handing you right off.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:43.340
I wonder if they were taking a note, though, from their arcade format and saying, well, you know, at the arcade, if you wanted to just resume where you were, you would just put in more quarters.

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:45.460
You know, you just say, yeah, I want to continue.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:50.600
And you get like, you know, I think 20 seconds or whatever to put in more quarters and stay right where you were.

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:58.760
And maybe they thought like, well, if you're going to spend $60 on the home version, you've earned your right to just have the unlimited continues, you know?

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:06.820
Yeah, yeah. I think what they would have been missing there, though, is that in the in that situation in the arcade, then the quarters become the consequence.

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:09.640
Like that's the it's almost part of the game design at that point.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:14.900
Right. If you're not going to be putting in quarters, you need some sort of consequence that makes you not want to die.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:19.620
I know that you lose points, but I know that we're just they don't matter.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:21.060
Yeah. First place. Yeah.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:29.560
And, you know, in thinking about modern games, you know, like you mentioned a second ago, like how modern games have enough going on.

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:39.440
I think that also, like if you think about like Uncharted, if it does something close to this to where like you die and you kind of end up pretty much right where you were.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.100
Or you might be back a couple enemies or whatever.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.940
Or you can go back to the beginning of the encounter.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:50.700
But but the but in Uncharted, that encounter is still challenging.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:52.460
Like you. Yeah. Yeah. You don't get punished.

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:53.640
We have to go really far back.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:57.320
But you get you have to still beat that encounter.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:00.520
You know, you still have to do it over and over again until you until you win.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:02.460
Whereas this, it's like you die.

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:04.540
You blink back in. You can just kind of keep running through.

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:06.480
Just keep dying. It literally puts you in the same spot.

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.280
Enemies still have the same amount of health.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:09.660
So you can just keep progressing forward. If you're the boss. Yeah.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:13.820
Like you can literally just almost ignore the enemies if you want and just keep dying.

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:16.980
Like it'll take you a long time, but you can do it.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:20.600
I mean, in that respect, Joe, you know, you could just keep running forward.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:23.140
Right. You don't even have to attack anybody.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.020
Yeah. I mean, you have to beat the bosses.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:32.760
But like, I think there's only certain sections where like progress is forcing you to kill somebody in order to move forward.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:36.300
Otherwise, it's like just keep, you know, just keep running along.

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:38.720
And to be honest, that's not a terrible strategy.

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:40.620
I don't know how everybody else plays.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:48.480
But in this kind of game where they give you so many enemies, I'm totally comfortable just killing what's in my way and letting everybody else just go free.

00:23:48.620 --> 00:24:00.400
You know? Yeah. I mean, I agree that that is probably the optimal way to finish the game, but it is so counterintuitive to me to see all this stuff and not shoot.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:09.000
Especially when it's so low, like low stakes, because I know that I'll always be able to come back.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:10.140
I don't know.

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:12.320
I just ended up trying to kill everyone.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:14.660
But did you also kill the prisoners?

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:21.280
I mean, accidentally, multiple times, most of the time, they look kind of similar.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:24.720
But I tried my best not to.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.160
But, you know, in the war, there's collateral damage.

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:30.300
Yeah, there's something about the way, and maybe it is what you said.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.180
Maybe they look close enough in color scheme or something.

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:39.320
I found myself killing way more prisoners of war than in any other, you know, rescue the prisoners game we've played yet.

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:48.340
I don't know what it is, but, like, maybe it's because there's so many enemies on screen that I'm just naturally inclined to be ready to shoot anything as it scrolls up to me.

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:56.960
But, like, I always, you know, I'm playing mostly in, like, the mid-front of the game, you know, like, as things are coming on.

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.100
And my natural instinct was just to shoot.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:03.960
And I think I just, I killed a lot of people that probably shouldn't have died.

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:11.140
I mean, and it doesn't help that some of your power-ups, like, there's, it's impossible not to hit them if there are enemies anywhere near them.

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:17.500
But did you guys notice that, like, the enemies in this game are kind of sneaky bastards?

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:25.100
Like, I, did you notice that they would sometimes look like they're surrendering and, like, stop shooting you?

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:28.140
And then they'd come back and just pull their gun out again?

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:31.920
Yeah, I was, I assumed that that's what they were doing.

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:33.600
And after a while, I was, like, are they surrendering?

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:35.420
Are they, like, waving for backup?

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:36.400
Or what are they doing?

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:41.900
Mike, am I you? No, yeah, I know exactly what you're saying.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:47.200
There's a lot of, I don't want to say confusing behavior from the AI.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:49.880
But, I mean, like, that is, it's all pretty cool stuff.

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:57.820
I was trying to figure out what those guys who, like, look like they're jumping on long pogo sticks that just kind of fly in from the sides.

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:01.580
Yeah, yeah. You know, like, I was trying to figure out what that's supposed to be.

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:05.880
Like, are they supposed to be jumping from a helicopter that we can't see or something?

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:09.260
Sometimes they even do it where it's, like, they're up further.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:14.540
They're up towards the top of the screen, which from the perspective we're playing is not up but forward.

00:26:14.740 --> 00:26:20.280
But then they, like, detach and, like, fall down the screen as if suddenly, like, they're on a wall.

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:21.940
It definitely is.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:24.680
But they put that little pogo stick thing on when they do that, too.

00:26:24.780 --> 00:26:26.260
So I don't know what it's supposed to represent.

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:38.640
It definitely is jumping because if you destroy a tank, sometimes instead of everybody in the tank dying, one person will jump out and try and fight you on foot.

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.840
So I think that's what they're doing there.

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:47.100
But, yeah, the whole forward also being up thing does not help with perspective.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.480
Yeah, and also to speak to, Sean, what you were saying about killing the hostages and stuff.

00:26:51.580 --> 00:27:00.280
I think that that, well, first of all, the reason that I accidentally killed them a lot was because I also realized that in this game you can just hold down both fire buttons.

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:03.340
So you can just be constantly throwing bombs and rapid firing.

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:07.480
And that would end up making the game a lot easier but also making me kill these enemies.

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:15.180
But I think that, like, what this game needs or when you're playing it personally is, like, you need to just set rules for yourself.

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:19.560
Because the game doesn't really, like, encourage you or, like, force you into doing it.

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:26.880
So if you, like, set the rule for yourself, like, hey, I want to save all the hostages or as many hostages as I can and I want to kill the enemies without dying.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:31.740
Like, those are all things that the game should enforce by punishing you in some way other than just points.

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:37.080
But since it doesn't, like, I feel like if you do play it that way, this game is actually pretty fun and pretty challenging.

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:45.040
Because, like, you have to think about things like you were saying how, like, a lot of your weapons will just kill everything in the area, including the hostages.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.840
Like, there were a few times when I was trying to do it this way where I'd have to think about, like,

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:51.800
I need to destroy this wall, but it'll kill these hostages.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:57.360
But if I, like, go far back enough and lob a bomb, like, close enough to the wall and kill the wall and not the hostage.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:27:59.940
Like, you have to really, like, strategize a little bit.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:05.100
And I think that makes this game, like, that's what this game both has and is missing.

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:06.940
In the sense that, like, it's all there.

00:28:06.940 --> 00:28:07.820
You can do that.

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:09.420
But, like, you have no reason to.

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:13.480
But, like, I think playing the game that way makes it more of a game.

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:15.160
Yeah, I agree.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:25.540
Like, this game is missing a lot of the higher level structure that usually, like, manifests in terms of, like, you know, the.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:29.780
Your, like, main objective in a game.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:32.600
Like, it's really just to go forward and kill everything.

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:36.160
But really there should be more at stake, like what you're saying.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:42.900
But on a more micro level, in terms of, like, how the game feels when you're.

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:51.720
Aside from the fact that this definitely would be helped by, like, the arcade control scheme of having a twin stick.

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.960
So that you can shoot in different directions that you're moving.

00:28:55.420 --> 00:29:00.640
Between how the game feels when you're shooting or when something explodes.

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:03.280
Or all those moment to moment things.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:05.340
They feel really good.

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:06.360
Yeah, definitely.

00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:08.100
I'll definitely back that up.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:11.600
That I think this is a game that you can have a lot of fun in the moment.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:15.180
And then, like, you know, if you're doing something like our show.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:17.100
If you think back on it.

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:19.860
You're like, well, how much fun actually was that?

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:21.780
Like, I question, you know.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:25.880
I think you should be able to just have fun playing a game and all that.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:27.960
But, like, how good actually was it?

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:32.960
And how much of it was just, like, finally a video game where you're kind of just unstoppable.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.660
And even though it's, like, a one-hit KO, it doesn't matter.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:37.580
Like, there's no stakes or anything like that.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.640
Like, it's almost like you're playing with cheat codes in a certain respect.

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:41.920
Right? Yeah, I'd say so.

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.560
Yeah. But, like, I still think that there's some value to it.

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:56.540
Like, the combination between those really abrasive sound effects for explosions and bullets flying.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:01.100
Like, it almost feels like the sound chip is malfunctioning.

00:30:01.100 --> 00:30:08.120
Combined with the slowdown, which is usually, like, a big problem for me.

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:13.640
It just, for some reason, I don't want to call this slowdown balanced.

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:20.960
But it felt so right when it's coupled with those sound effects and just all this mayhem and all these enemies.

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:34.060
It really felt like that old school experience that I always thought was just sort of rose-colored glasses that modern retro games tried to...

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:38.960
Like, I always bring up Luftrausers as a game that does this.

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:50.380
But those experiences of just this really chunky action feeling that I'm really having trouble articulating.

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:56.560
But moment to moment, this is finally that experience that I didn't think really existed.

00:30:57.140 --> 00:31:03.900
Well, I mean, look no further than after you defeat a boss and the screen just goes into epilepsy mania.

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:05.360
Absolutely. So awesome.

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:11.580
Even I thought I was going to have something happen to me when it was flashing that many times and that fast.

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:13.200
Like, it feels good.

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:17.280
The bosses, again, like, you know, knowing that you have the Unlimited continues and everything like that, like, whatever.

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:27.660
But, like, the bosses themselves are just, like, satisfying big enough deals to take down that, like, again, that moment-to-moment gameplay, like, presentation is top-notch here.

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:37.000
And, you know, as far as, like, difficulty and, you know, going back to it, you know, how that all feels.

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:50.020
This is the first game and maybe the only game I could think of where the manual tells you about, like, how to do the level select and difficulty selection, like, cheat code, if you will, right inside the manual.

00:31:50.800 --> 00:32:05.120
I've never seen that before, but they do tell you that if you just press A and B, hold down A and B, or, sorry, hold down start and A or B, that you'll be able to choose whichever area you want to go to.

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:11.400
Because they completely understand that the game has, you know, no stakes to it to begin with because you can always just pick up where you want.

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:22.220
But also you can change the level of difficulty a little bit, and that's not something I did, but I'd be interested to see, like, all right, well, if they're going to continue to give me the unlimited continues, maybe I should beef up the difficulty on the game.

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:29.260
Yeah, you know, I never, this is one of the rare games that I didn't read the manual, so I actually didn't know that.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:36.000
I just sort of jumped right in, but maybe I'll play it again to see what the experience is like.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:41.500
Yeah, also one of the slightly less rare for me games that I also didn't read the manual.

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:45.620
Probably should have. I just think that's funny to include, like, the level select code.

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:49.940
Like, you know, the manual's telling you just go to the end if you want.

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:57.840
Like, if you want, if it's your literally, you know, you're on your way home from Babbage's or Funkoland or wherever you bought video games from back then,

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:02.620
and you're reading the manual and you're like, oh, hey, I'm just going to go right to the end of the game.

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:07.960
This time I could safely say that you won't suffer any consequences for that, right?

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:14.120
Would you feel cheated in this game if you just started playing at the palace level from the get-go?

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:16.580
Like, would that feel like you didn't get the true experience?

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:22.120
You didn't learn what it was like to go through the jungle and the city and you cheated your way to the palace?

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:24.100
I don't know. I don't think anybody would agree with that.

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:26.760
I also don't think anyone would have too much fun with that.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:28.260
Yeah. No, sure, yeah.

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:31.880
Fun, sure. But I guess what I'm saying is, like, you know, in a modern-day video game, right,

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:38.060
it's crazy that, you know, like, some people will just never get to the end, right?

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:42.960
And there's no way to get to the end unless you play the entire game, right?

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:48.920
So Dragon Quest XI is, like, this 100-hour game, and I played, like, 40 hours of it,

00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:54.600
but I'll never know how it ends, and there's no, like, option in the menu for me to be, like, go to the ending.

00:33:54.920 --> 00:33:59.320
But in a book, I can just say I'm done with this book, but I want to know how it ends,

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:02.860
and I can just go to the last, like, 20 pages and read it.

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:04.440
It might not make that much sense.

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:05.360
You've talked about that before.

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:14.300
I've heard this argument a lot, and I don't have, like, a really strong argument against why people shouldn't be able to do that,

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:16.580
but I am very strongly against it anyways.

00:34:16.740 --> 00:34:17.600
I don't know why.

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:19.200
I just don't like the idea of that.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:23.800
Are you against, like, for video games specifically, or are you saying, like, no, don't even do that in the book?

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:25.780
Like, you don't deserve to read the end of the book.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:27.120
I mean, personally, like, that's a person, whatever.

00:34:27.240 --> 00:34:28.240
Like, you can do whatever you want.

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:29.460
Like, obviously, that's fine.

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:33.140
Like, I just think, yeah, you ruin the book if you're just going to read the end of the book.

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:37.720
But I think with video games, it's like, it depends on the game, too.

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:42.100
Like, if it's like a, I mean, I don't know.

00:34:42.140 --> 00:34:43.440
Like I said, I don't have a good argument.

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:45.360
No, okay. I've got a good argument.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:58.520
You know, I think that if developers start putting that in their games, me personally, while I'm playing, I'll feel, like, I'll always in the back of my head be like, well, yeah, like, it's cool that I did this, but, like, I could have just gotten there by skipping it if I really wanted to.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:01.220
Like, that bothers me that that, like, option exists.

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:06.460
Plus, I like having the, like, camaraderie with someone else being like, oh, I finally beat this hard thing.

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:07.420
It's like, oh, I beat that, too.

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:09.760
But, like, now you lose that, you know?

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:20.040
Or I also like the, like, idea of, like, hey, there are games where, like, I might not see the ending and, like, I have to, like, weight the value of, like, how badly do I want to get to the end of this game?

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:27.220
Like, you know what I mean? So here's where I think I can categorically say that you're wrong, Mike.

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:47.700
So with a book, with a movie, with a TV show, with any form of entertainment, music, it's always, like, an author telling you something or showing you something or playing music to you something.

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:58.300
Whereas video games are the only major form of entertainment that are based around the idea of user agency.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:09.040
And I think that that is what makes skipping to the end of something counter to the reason that the art exists.

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:14.120
Why that isn't really, that's intended.

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:19.180
Like, you're supposed to, like, the reason the game exists is for you to experience it.

00:36:20.600 --> 00:36:26.520
And you two actually have an effect on the game and for the game to have an effect on you directly.

00:36:26.660 --> 00:36:34.660
Not so much indirectly, like, oh, how, like, oh, this album changed my life or, like, this book really spoke to me.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:38.300
Like, it's actually you doing the game.

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:42.520
I appreciate the conversation that we have going here.

00:36:42.520 --> 00:36:47.500
I just can't help but laugh because the only reason, like, somehow this got to this level.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:56.580
But I just want to say that I brought this all up as a point to say that the final level of this game isn't really all that much different than the first level.

00:36:56.720 --> 00:37:03.540
And so, therefore, like, skipping around in stages doesn't really feel that consequential in a guerrilla war.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:11.180
I wouldn't say that I would even agree that, like, you know, God of War 2018 on PS4, right?

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:13.860
It would be kind of stupid to just go to the end.

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:25.360
Like, they're telling you a story and the gameplay evolves and you unlock a lot of, you know, like, a lot of things in your skill tree to become a better player and also to become a stronger Kratos, right?

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:28.200
Like, all that makes sense in a modern game.

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:31.200
And, you know, this argument could probably apply to a lot of NES games.

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:32.640
But I'm talking specifically about Guerrilla War.

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:37.180
I just didn't feel that much of a difference level to level other than aesthetically.

00:37:37.340 --> 00:37:40.000
Like, I just didn't notice, like, oh, I'm in the palace.

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:42.280
Like, you know, it's a lot harder now.

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:44.460
This game's really getting to me now.

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:45.700
I'm dying a lot more often.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:47.680
Like, I don't know. Just about the same as the beginning.

00:37:47.680 --> 00:37:48.520
I don't know.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:51.500
I think each level has its own different take.

00:37:51.740 --> 00:37:57.220
Like, earlier in the game, like, it's not just a different setting.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:00.680
Like, there are different layouts.

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:11.620
Like, there's a middle – I forgot which level it was, but in the middle it almost turns into more of a labyrinth of crossing this river over and over and trying to get to –

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:22.900
Yeah, trying to get to the hostages that are sort of tucked away in these strange nooks and having to deal with crossing the river over and over.

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:31.260
And in the city level, there's a lot more choke points, I feel.

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:45.920
Like, there's a lot of situations where the entire corridor of the city street is blocked off in a way that you sort of have to first deal with the guys on the roof and then deal with the people up the road.

00:38:46.060 --> 00:38:53.120
And I think that there are different layouts where each level feels different, maybe not so much harder.

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:58.980
And also, there are levels where you can get into a tank and then that idea goes away.

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:02.980
Anyway, so, yeah, I think each level has a different feel to it.

00:39:03.100 --> 00:39:07.840
Not so much, like, a difficulty curve to it, but I think they have a different feel.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:14.540
Yeah, I would say the same thing, that they have a different feel, but, yeah, the difficulty never ramps up much.

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:21.600
But, yeah, even, like, the sewer level feels like, you know, you're always in this, like, in the water half the time.

00:39:21.760 --> 00:39:25.240
And then I think this might – I'm not sure if this is the one you were talking about where it's like you're in the water half the time

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:29.940
and then you go over to the sides to get the enemies if you feel like it or to get the –

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.060
If that was above ground, yeah.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:32.960
Okay, yeah, there's that one too, yeah.

00:39:34.660 --> 00:39:41.860
But there's one thing that – one level we're not talking about, which is just one random level in the middle of the game

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:44.500
where you're just, like, on, like, a railroad.

00:39:45.020 --> 00:39:46.140
You're, like, in a cart.

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:47.700
It's like a bonus level.

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:54.720
It's like an auto-scroller and you have to, like, lasso passages from your left into your right.

00:39:55.460 --> 00:39:56.280
That was weird.

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:58.800
But that is really – I think, Sean, you just said it.

00:39:58.840 --> 00:40:00.140
It's more like a bonus stage, right?

00:40:00.140 --> 00:40:01.120
It's like a point –

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:02.680
Yeah, there's no one on me.

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:06.720
Right? Like, yeah, you're just trying to, like, you know, raise your high score and everything like that.

00:40:06.720 --> 00:40:08.600
I guess that's true. I appreciated that, though.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:09.500
It helped break it. Yeah.

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:10.820
It helped break up the game a little bit.

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:14.540
When I first saw it, I kind of thought, like, oh, this is going to be one of those kind of games

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:17.700
where they throw in these other little things once in a while where it's like, oh, now you're doing this.

00:40:17.760 --> 00:40:20.160
Now you're flying a helicopter. But, like, they never do anything like that again.

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:21.620
So it's just that one level.

00:40:24.280 --> 00:40:29.360
And one other little fun note about that level is – well, I don't know if I'd call it fun,

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:31.760
but for some reason, you know, you're moving.

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:33.180
You get this nice sense of speed.

00:40:33.320 --> 00:40:37.460
But when you turn a corner, the whole game just slows down so much.

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:40.100
Oh, yeah. Turning a corner, it's like you just come to a halt.

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.000
And it takes, like, three seconds just to, like, rotate the cart.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:44.240
Because it shows you rotating.

00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:49.000
Yeah. And, like, at that point, if you're going to have that much trouble with making the game turn,

00:40:49.320 --> 00:40:51.300
just have it go straight the whole level.

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:52.820
It's not that long of a level anyways.

00:40:53.020 --> 00:40:57.760
Just to go back to the idea of, like, you know, the stages playing out a little differently

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:02.460
and, you know, concepts kind of disappearing between levels,

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:05.560
I think that's just another benefit here, though, of having –

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:10.300
especially in a short-burst-style arcade action game where, you know,

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:14.220
each particular stage might not take you more than, I don't know,

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:16.400
what, less than five minutes to clear through.

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:20.560
You know, that's just another benefit of having a level selection thing.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:25.400
And I think more games should be inclined to tell you if they exist in their manuals

00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:26.660
and to be able to do them.

00:41:26.920 --> 00:41:31.240
Because, you know, you might just want to go back and revisit, you know,

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:33.420
the city stage or the sewer stage.

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:35.140
You know, I don't think you have to –

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.140
I think it's a consequence of games like this

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:41.180
where you have to start from the beginning each time that you –

00:41:42.640 --> 00:41:46.160
each time you start your game up because there is no save function, right?

00:41:46.160 --> 00:41:50.320
So, you know, you can't make multiple different save files and be like,

00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:53.720
oh, I'm going to save here each time and make my own kind of, like, level select screen.

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:57.640
I think it's important that these games have some kind of level selection,

00:41:57.640 --> 00:42:00.300
especially when they can just be short bursts of fun, right?

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:05.520
I don't think anybody really back then was sitting down for, you know,

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:07.940
hours at a time playing video games.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:11.000
I think that's become a thing over time as games have gotten longer.

00:42:12.100 --> 00:42:19.880
Yeah, I still think you should play the game along the level flow first.

00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:24.840
And then, yeah, if you find a level that you just want to play over and over

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:29.140
because it just feels that good to you, then no, go for it.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:30.700
Well, here's the thing about that.

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:33.440
This is just – maybe I'm going off on a little bit of a tangent here too,

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:39.400
but, like, I fully agree with Sean that, like, yeah, you should play that way.

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:45.320
But the argument that I can't come up with a good argument against is when someone says,

00:42:45.460 --> 00:42:48.040
well, yeah, that's what you think, but if somebody else paid for the game

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:50.360
and wants to play it that way, why shouldn't they be able to?

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:52.600
And, like, I don't have a great argument at that.

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:54.180
No, no, no. I just don't think they should be able to.

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:59.660
Yeah, I mean, I'm totally for telling people how to enjoy their products.

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:03.640
There's a right way and there's a wrong way, and you're doing it wrong.

00:43:04.300 --> 00:43:05.880
We do it every week on this podcast.

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:09.120
Yeah. We tell people that they're wrong for liking Zelda 2.

00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:10.000
Exactly. Oh, no.

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:12.460
Yeah, you should have spent your money on something else back then.

00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:17.300
This is where I disagree. You know, we've gotten some comments about Zelda 2.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:22.280
It is – I want you people to know that are commenting that we should have Zelda 2 on the essential

00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:24.280
games list that you're my people, okay?

00:43:24.280 --> 00:43:25.420
We all need to band together.

00:43:25.540 --> 00:43:28.780
We can overthrow the people keeping it off.

00:43:28.780 --> 00:43:34.040
It is the official position of this institution, the podcast nostalgia,

00:43:34.040 --> 00:43:37.800
that Zelda 2 is not an essential game.

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:41.040
Which is what proves to me that the institution is flawed.

00:43:42.380 --> 00:43:45.860
And we will stack the courts if we have to to make sure that it stays that way.

00:43:48.320 --> 00:43:53.260
Next thing I wanted to talk about while we're still talking about some form of this game is,

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:56.360
you know, we had briefly mentioned the tanks.

00:43:57.380 --> 00:44:03.820
And since that is like a really cool and fun way to take an already action-heavy game

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:06.760
and make it even more like you have all the power in the world,

00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:11.900
using the tanks, you know, they have like a – I think they say in on them.

00:44:12.080 --> 00:44:13.240
And I don't know.

00:44:13.360 --> 00:44:14.620
My dumb brain couldn't figure out –

00:44:14.620 --> 00:44:16.220
You couldn't either until –

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:18.120
Yeah. I was like, oh, all right.

00:44:18.220 --> 00:44:20.780
So I'll go in. And finally I did.

00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:22.680
And, you know, thanks to the manual.

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:28.740
And I got to say, you know, like the tanks, you know, they're usually towards the ends of the level.

00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:32.300
So it feels like they want you to have one for the boss fights.

00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:34.980
But, you know, I just think they're great.

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:35.960
I think they're a great addition.

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:40.620
And they completely stole that idea from their own other game, Akari Warriors.

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:45.900
But in Akari Warriors, the tanks are like little toys compared to the tank that you got in this game.

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:52.880
I like in this that when the tank is like low on health, it sort of gives you a warning.

00:44:53.140 --> 00:44:56.200
And I thought that that was just like, oh, one more hit and the tank goes away.

00:44:56.420 --> 00:45:02.220
But no, like I think not only just if you get hit one more time, but if you just wait too long,

00:45:02.560 --> 00:45:04.240
the tank will explode and you die.

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:06.540
Like you have to make the decision to get out of the tank.

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:11.620
Yeah. Correct. I think one major missed opportunity there, though, which I thought was going to work,

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:14.980
was I thought I would, because it was beeping, giving me that warning I knew it was going to explode.

00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:19.540
I thought I'd be able to leave the tank in a cluster of enemies and have that explosion kill them.

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:23.900
Oh. And it doesn't hurt the enemies, which I thought was just a real missed opportunity.

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:28.280
Because that would have been kind of epic a couple of times when it was like about to blow.

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:39.100
So, but yeah, I will say that the tank feels like an appropriate like power boost for being in a tank

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:44.200
without giving you like too much God status if you were to ignore the fact that you already have a God status anyways

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:45.000
because you can't die.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:49.160
But, you know, it's like, I think it's a good balance.

00:45:49.460 --> 00:45:55.800
You not being able to die, though, actually makes sense in the context of why, you know,

00:45:55.880 --> 00:46:00.760
why the Cubans would only send two people to take on an entire government.

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:04.080
You know, it's like, well, it turns out actually they can't die, so they're our best bet.

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:05.480
Why expend anybody?

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:09.640
I think that they are dying and they're just constantly getting replaced by like an understudy

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:11.340
who's just like following right behind them.

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:14.680
It's like another guy runs in from off screen after you die.

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:20.460
Yeah. It's a very unique form of warfare where you just send one guy in at a time and hope for the best.

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:22.680
Yeah. Well, think about it.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:27.980
If one guy takes down three, eventually, you know, the odds are in your favor over time.

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:30.480
Yeah. I love it.

00:46:32.060 --> 00:46:35.780
One more thing about the tanks not, you know, not killing people when they explode.

00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:38.760
That happens in a lot of things in this game, though, right?

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:41.960
Like the mine, not the mines, but I guess they are mines.

00:46:42.220 --> 00:46:47.480
Yeah. They don't, they don't, like if an enemy touches them, they don't blow up.

00:46:47.620 --> 00:46:48.580
It's like, what gives?

00:46:49.560 --> 00:46:52.680
I think that if you shoot the mine, it should explode.

00:46:53.780 --> 00:46:55.920
Right. That's our position.

00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:59.420
And yet, here we are. Yeah, that's our official position.

00:46:59.420 --> 00:47:00.740
We can agree on that.

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.300
That's for sure. We didn't vote for that yet, though.

00:47:02.520 --> 00:47:10.180
We didn't. You know, another thing, just because we're just obviously spitballing at this point, just

00:47:10.180 --> 00:47:11.800
things as we can remember them.

00:47:12.140 --> 00:47:17.060
I went back and played Commando, and I was shocked to find out that you can be blocked

00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:21.340
by the palm trees from the tops of the palm trees.

00:47:21.500 --> 00:47:23.360
And that doesn't make any sense to me graphically.

00:47:23.360 --> 00:47:24.700
Graphically. Oh, yeah.

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:29.680
That like, you know, like the top of the tree, because it's on a 2D sprite, that it has a

00:47:29.680 --> 00:47:30.960
collision zone with you.

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:34.840
I'm just glad that, you know, the days of programming have gotten better.

00:47:34.940 --> 00:47:37.540
We've already established that. I think all those trees are laying down on their side.

00:47:37.540 --> 00:47:40.200
We've already established that up is also forward.

00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:07.520
So let's move over to sequels and spinoffs, of which there are none.

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:11.960
And with that, we can go into the biggest part of the show.

00:48:12.080 --> 00:48:19.800
The reason we do the show, we vote on whether Guerrilla War is an essential game on the essential

00:48:19.800 --> 00:48:24.680
games list. Joe, you're up first.

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:31.740
Other than your vote for Guerrilla War, I would also like you to take an official stance on the

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:34.100
palm tree collision zone detection.

00:48:34.440 --> 00:48:37.240
Okay. Whether or not I like it or not, right?

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:39.860
I forgot. Or whether or not you're for it or not.

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:49.680
Okay. So to start with that, although I like that they tried to, you know, make these palm trees

00:48:49.680 --> 00:48:54.260
and make them, you know, part of the environment, give you a little more immersion, I don't like

00:48:54.260 --> 00:49:01.100
the fact that the palms, which should be in the air above your head, hit you as if they're

00:49:01.100 --> 00:49:04.120
on the ground. So I'm going to vote no, I am not for that.

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:06.960
Thank you. What was the other thing I'm supposed to vote for?

00:49:07.640 --> 00:49:10.280
Oh, it's whether Guerrilla War is, yeah.

00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:11.420
Essential games, that's right.

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:17.620
So I think that, I think that this, like I said earlier, this game had a little bit of a higher

00:49:17.620 --> 00:49:19.520
bar for me because we've played things like this.

00:49:19.660 --> 00:49:22.780
I think it did a great job in a lot of aspects.

00:49:22.780 --> 00:49:28.740
But the things that like, that it did well, which, you know, I mentioned earlier that,

00:49:28.740 --> 00:49:33.560
you know, it gives me this feeling that it's like a little more attainable to be able to

00:49:33.560 --> 00:49:38.600
dodge things and to be able to be good at this game is completely negated by the fact that

00:49:38.600 --> 00:49:40.680
it doesn't matter if you're good at this game.

00:49:41.380 --> 00:49:46.160
You know, I mean, you, it doesn't matter if I can dodge bullets because getting hit by

00:49:46.160 --> 00:49:48.880
bullets doesn't do anything, you know, fundamentally.

00:49:48.880 --> 00:49:54.000
Now, I realize that we have completely ignored the fact that some people do like to play for

00:49:54.000 --> 00:49:57.040
high scores. And that does make a difference if you're playing for the high score.

00:49:57.160 --> 00:50:01.220
That's almost like what I was talking about earlier, where that makes you want to save

00:50:01.220 --> 00:50:05.740
all the hostages and not accidentally kill anyone and, and kill, you know, as many of

00:50:05.740 --> 00:50:06.620
the enemies as you can.

00:50:07.660 --> 00:50:09.840
So like, maybe the game is better for people like that.

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:15.520
But I, but I do think that just overall, it's just, it's the, it's major flaw being that

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:18.500
there's no consequences is just too big for me.

00:50:18.880 --> 00:50:21.200
To, to consider putting it on the essential games list.

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:23.460
So I'm going to say no. Sean, your vote.

00:50:24.700 --> 00:50:32.920
You know, I, I know that this game has some problems in terms of, uh, its balance and

00:50:33.660 --> 00:50:37.700
specifically the whole, uh, you never lose progress thing.

00:50:37.700 --> 00:50:45.520
Um, but in terms of the top down shooter, this is the most fun I've ever had playing one.

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:51.780
And, uh, I didn't think that I ever would have fun playing them, especially with like

00:50:51.780 --> 00:50:58.140
the, the natural handicap of the NES of always shooting straight forward or straight wherever

00:50:58.140 --> 00:50:59.340
direction you're walking in.

00:50:59.940 --> 00:51:03.840
Um, it, it just feels too good to play.

00:51:03.880 --> 00:51:08.540
And I, I feel like I'll be the only vote for it, but I have to put it there for posterity.

00:51:08.780 --> 00:51:14.020
Um, I would vote guerrilla war, uh, to be placed on the essential games list.

00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:19.960
And, and with that, uh, Mike, in addition to your vote for the essential games list, I

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:25.280
would also like your vote as to whether, uh, the current Cuban regime is legitimate.

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:28.840
Thank you very much, uh, for that.

00:51:29.060 --> 00:51:32.640
Because now you've given me, um, two tough decisions to make.

00:51:32.640 --> 00:51:38.780
One, I have to make a stance about a, uh, third, uh, a, uh, you know, a foreign government

00:51:38.780 --> 00:51:41.600
of which, you know, I'm sure they, they listen to the podcast.

00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:42.640
I'm confident of it.

00:51:42.640 --> 00:51:44.300
I think one of the Castros does.

00:51:44.920 --> 00:51:51.660
Right. So I'm confident that my response will be noted, but also that I, uh, you know, am a,

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:53.020
am the vote here. Essentially.

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:55.520
If I say yay, then it's on the essential games.

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:57.220
If I say nay, it's not.

00:51:58.340 --> 00:52:02.500
Here's, here's the crazy twist in, in the whole, in the whole thing.

00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:04.860
I had a great time playing guerrilla war.

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:07.200
I really liked this game.

00:52:07.420 --> 00:52:13.180
Uh, without a doubt, I would recommend it, uh, to my fellow NES, uh, game players.

